#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069547} :activity/id #uuid "7b10485d-7fa4-5254-83ce-37fba428298f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050089 :topic/id #uuid "2cb07e2e-6475-548a-8275-28e63ed7fbf9" :topic/math-academy-id 460} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069547 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069956 :tutorial/content "The **circumference** of a circle is the length of the path that goes around the circle.\n\n![](images/ac/ac8f31a99961c51cb53695d9daab017126d1316b720e6383b876824d7c7947bd.png)\n\nThe circumference is usually denoted by the letter $C.$\n\nOne important fact about the circumference is that, for *any* circle, no matter how small or large it is, the ratio of circumference $C$ to diameter $d$ is a fixed number! Specifically,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{C}{d} = {\\color{blue}\\boldsymbol{\\pi}} \\approx 3.141\\,592\\,654...\n$$\n\n \n\nThis important irrational number is called **pi** (pronounced \"pie\") and we use the Greek letter $\\pi$ to express it. So, we can calculate the circumference by using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C=\\pi d.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, our circle has diameter $4\\,\\textrm{cm},$ so its circumference is\n\n$$\nC = \\pi \\cdot 4 = 4\\pi \\approx 12.566\\,\\mathrm{cm},\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal digits.\n\nSince the diameter is twice as long as the radius ($d=2r$), we can also write the formula for the circumference as\n\n$$\nC = \\pi d = 2\\pi r.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e75a0c81-7a99-51d8-85ff-de6951125d7b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2423 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3437ac3b-f6ef-5e78-8b46-bca7d485abd4" :step/math-academy-id 10876 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069548}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069548 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068681 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068542 :question/id #uuid "ba3d3abd-4b93-5705-8fcb-cb88b58dad2f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3178"} :knowledge-poi